{"id": "section-body-22831", "title": "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty-one", "blocks": [{"id": "body-22831", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)", "runs": [{"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Kidney"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Injury"}, {"text": " (AKI)"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "666"}}, {"id": "body-22832", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Acute kidney injury is a sudden decline in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) resulting in elevation of serum blood urea, creatinine and other waste products. Practically AKI is recognized when there is urine output of less than 0.5ml/kg/hour for >6 hours. Causes of AKI are classified as pre-renal, renal and post-renal causes and usually result in reversible kidney failure, these causes include;", "runs": [{"text": "Acute kidney injury is a sudden decline in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) resulting in elevation of serum blood urea, creatinine and other waste products. Practically AKI is recognized when there is urine output of less than 0.5ml/kg/hour for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": ">6 hours. Causes of AKI are classified as pre-renal, renal and post-renal causes and usually result in reversible kidney failure, these causes include;"}]}, {"id": "body-22834", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Table 21.15: Classification, Causes and Management of Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)", "runs": [{"text": "Table"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "21.15:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Classification,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Causes"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Kidney"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Injury"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(AKI)"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-22835", "type": "table", "rows": [{"cells": [{"blocks": [{"id": "body-22835-r1-c1-00002", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Tab